In practice, CV partitions models into three compressibility classes: \emph{uniform} (CV$\,{<}\,$0.1; safe for moderate pruning but method-sensitive), \emph{graded} (CV$\,{\approx}\,$0.15--0.22; threshold-dependent with method-independent degradation), and \emph{catastrophic} (CV$\,{>}\,$0.3; no pruning tolerance). This coarse classification, validated on four closed-loop architectures, provides actionable guidance without requiring downstream evaluation.

\textbf{Method$\,{\times}\,$task interaction.} Extending to attention-based scoring on X-VLA ($k{=}0.5$) reveals that scoring method effectiveness is itself task-dependent: last-layer attention achieves 90.2\% on Long ($+$45\,pp over L2's 45.0\%) but collapses to ${\sim}$0\% on Goal, where L2 retains 61.0\%. Sequential multi-step tasks benefit from attention's focus on decoder-relevant tokens; goal-conditioned reasoning requires spatial precision that attention weights do not encode. No single scoring function dominates across task types (Appendix~\ref{app:attention_pruning}).

\subsection{VTR Recovery}

The primary contribution of VTR is recovering functional performance where all deletion methods yield 0\%, not matching the official baseline.

\subsection{Attention-Based Scoring}
\label{app:attention_pruning}

Table~\ref{tab:attention_pruning} compares attention-based token scoring with L2 norm and random selection on X-VLA at $k{=}0.5$ (25 of 50 tokens retained). Three attention variants are evaluated: last-layer attention (attn\_last), mean attention across all decoder layers (attn\_mean), and attention rollout~\cite{abnar2020quantifying}.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{X-VLA success rates (\%) at $k{=}0.5$ across five scoring methods. Attention-based methods excel on Object and Long but collapse on Goal, where L2 norm selection retains 61.0\%. Bold indicates best per suite.}
\label{tab:attention_pruning}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Suite & attn\_last & attn\_mean & attn\_rollout & L2 & Random \\
\midrule
Object & \textbf{99.2} & 98.4 & 97.4 & 42.6 & 70.6 \\
Long & \textbf{90.2} & -- & -- & 45.0 & 29.6 \\
Goal & ${\sim}$0 & ${\sim}$0 & ${\sim}$0 & \textbf{61.0} & 0.0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The method$\,{\times}\,$task reversal is striking: attention-based scoring outperforms L2 by 45\,pp on Long (90.2\% vs.\ 45.0\%) but underperforms by ${\sim}$61\,pp on Goal (${\sim}$0\% vs.\ 61.0\%). On Object, all three attention variants achieve near-baseline performance (${\geq}$97.4\%), far exceeding both L2 (42.6\%) and random (70.6\%).

The reversal has a functional explanation. Long-horizon tasks involve sequential manipulation steps where the decoder's attention naturally focuses on task-relevant tokens at each step; attention scores capture this step-by-step relevance. Goal-conditioned tasks require reasoning about spatial relationships between the current state and a goal image; this demands precise spatial encoding that attention weights---which reflect decoder query patterns, not spatial information content---do not capture. L2 norms, as a proxy for activation magnitude, better preserve spatially informative tokens for goal-conditioned reasoning.

